  "full_response": "Kubernetes handles pod scheduling and resource management primarily through the use of schedulers, resource requests and limits, and various other mechanisms designed to ensure that pods are placed on appropriate nodes and that CPU and memory resources are utilized efficiently. Here’s a breakdown of the key processes:\n\n### 1. Pod Scheduling:\nKubernetes uses schedulers to manage the placement of pods across the available nodes in the cluster. Initially, all pods in Kubernetes are unscheduled until a scheduler assigns them to appropriate nodes based on resource availability and constraints specified by the user.\n\n- **Scheduler Components**:\n  - **Scheduler General**: Handles the instance scheduling where the task (in most cases, the pod) is placed on a node with enough capacity while respecting constraints.\n  - **Scheduler with Affinity and Anti-Affinity Rules**: Allows users to specify rules for pod placement, such as affinity (encouraging or requiring certain pod placement) and anti-affinity (avoiding certain pod placements).\n  - **Priority and Preemption**: When multiple pods compete for limited resources, the priority of the pods a critical role. High-priority pods can preempt (replace) lower-priority ones on a node with insufficient resources.\n\n### 2. Resource Requests and Limits:\nResource management at the container level is controlled through resource requests and limits, which can be specified in the pod definition.\n\n- **Resource Requests**:\n  - **CPU Request**: The guaranteed amount of CPU that the container will use, measured in CPU shares.\n  - **Memory Request**: The guaranteed amount of memory usage, measured in Kilobytes (KiB).\n  - **Node Affinity**: Ensures the pod will get placed on a node that matches the specified criteria.\n  \n- **Resource Limits**:\n  - **CPU Limit**: The maximum amount of CPU that the container can use, which can also be expressed in milliCPU units.\n  - **Memory Limit**: The maximum amount of memory that the container can use, measured in Kilobytes (KiB).\n  - **Requests** and **Limits** together: Requests ensure the described resource levels are available, while limits ensure that the container cannot use more than specified amounts. Pods can only be scheduled on nodes that can satisfy the resource requests, and no resource over-utilization will occur.\n\n- **Effects of Defaults**:\n - If no resource requests are specified, the Kubernetes system uses default values.\n  - Example: CPU shares (default 1024), and memory limit (default",
